## Parent and existing owners

Child application of [APPAREL-ENG-TWIN-001 #11731](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/11731). Compose:
- [PERSONAL-GARMENT-STUDIO-001 #12093](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/12093): wearer-authored design intent, editable choices and constraints.
- [EVERYDAY-WEAR-001 #12157](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/12157): comfort, durability, care and indoor/outdoor use.
- [WARDROBE-FIRST-OPTIMIZATION-001 #12094](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/12094): existing wardrobe, repair/alteration and acquisition alternatives.
- [APPAREL-INTEROP-001 #11750](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/11750): pattern/3D semantic preservation.
- [SEWING-PLAN-COMPILER #12134](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/12134): typed construction plan.
- [TEXTILE-HYG-MFG-000 #5984](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/issues/5984): textile/garment function and wash qualification.
- [Sol-Atlas apparel spatial projection #738](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/sol-atlas-leptos/issues/738), Symtropy cloth profile #456 and Mycelix textile DPP projection #6092.

This is a product-family requirements and benchmark issue, not a new garment CAD, travel planner, textile solver or universal wardrobe score.

## Mission

Create a **modern / post-futurist travel robe** that is elegant, exceptionally comfortable, practical, and adaptable to the realities of travel. It should feel like a considered garment rather than a costume, dressing gown, hotel robe or gadget-laden techwear product.

The design should be plural and user-authored: robe, wrap coat, long overshirt, kaftan-influenced silhouette or other directions may be explored without treating any one culture's ceremonial garment as an interchangeable aesthetic resource. References and motifs require rights/respectful provenance and user intent.

The robe is a non-PPE apparel product. It is not thermal protective clothing, a sleep product claim, medical clothing, or guaranteed travel-security equipment.

## Existing commercial comparators — category novelty is not claimed

Travel wraps and changing robes already exist. MERSEA's Modern Travel Wrap, for example, advertises a securing loop and matching travel bag; Dryrobe's Tech-Lite changing robe advertises 1 kg packed-with-bag mass, weatherproof construction and packability. These are not direct equivalents (wrap versus weather/changing robe), but they establish that travel-oriented wrap/robe features are not novel by themselves. Compare exact garments by intended use, coverage, warmth, price, mass, packed volume, seated comfort, closure/pocket function, care and durability.

- MERSEA Modern Travel Wrap: https://mersea.com/products/modern-travel-wrap
- Dryrobe Tech-Lite: https://dryrobe.com/products/dryrobe-lite

## Product thesis

One coherent garment should support several ordinary travel states:
- at home and in accommodation;
- airport/station waiting and lounge time;
- seated aircraft/train/bus journeys;
- walking through terminals and cities;
- hotel arrival, rest and changing layers;
- cool indoor environments and variable outdoor conditions.

The garment should transition gracefully without becoming too hot, bulky, revealing, restrictive, noisy, fragile or difficult to manage. It must remain comfortable when sitting and moving, not only when standing for a photograph.

## Design language

Explore a restrained, elegant, futuristic visual language:
- clean continuous silhouettes and carefully controlled drape;
- architectural collar/neckline and shoulder geometry;
- asymmetry or modular wrap closure only when it improves use;
- hidden but reachable pockets;
- clean seam architecture and minimal visible hardware;
- optional integrated scarf/hood/collar variants, not mandatory features;
- subtle adjustability that does not make the garment look like outdoor equipment;
- inclusive, user-chosen lengths, coverage, fit and layering.
Avoid overcomplication, dangling cords, loud techwear clichés, fragile decorative components and pockets that distort the garment's drape.

## Required user choices

- silhouette and length; open, wrap, belted, concealed or alternative closure;
- coverage/modesty and neckline/collar preference;
- sleeve type and cuff adjustment;
- fit/ease by posture and layering thickness;
- pocket map (secure passport/phone/wallet, quick-access boarding items, internal valuables, hand-warming where desired);
- venting, slits, movement gussets and hem management;
- material hand, weight, drape, breathability, stretch, opacity and noise;
- temperature/climate envelope and indoor/outdoor transition frequency;
- wash/dry, wrinkle, odour and maintenance constraints;
- accessible one-hand closure and left/right-hand access preferences;
- price ceiling, repairability and target service life;
- personal aesthetic, cultural reference and asset-rights declarations.

No body photo, gender label or cultural inference should be required. Preserve editable manual fit and measurement paths.

## Candidate architecture — hypotheses to test, not prescribed winners

- A soft, breathable next-to-skin or lining interface where useful, with a low-friction outer drape.
- A light, stable main textile chosen by measured thermal, moisture, pilling, abrasion, opacity and care behavior—not fibre marketing alone.
- A removable or convertible warmth layer rather than one heavy all-season fabric.
- Secure pocket construction that does not swing, print through, pull the robe open or create uncomfortable pressure when seated.
- A closure system that stays secure while sitting, walking and carrying luggage, and can be opened without awkward two-handed manipulation.
- Sleeve/hem geometry that avoids catching on handles, wheels, steps or chair hardware.
- Venting that can be adjusted without creating exposed fragile openings.
- Repairable hems, cuffs, pocket bags and closures; avoid unnecessary bonded/mixed-material construction.
- The electronics-free, offline, low-maintenance design is the default. Do not add sensors or connectivity absent a demonstrated user need.

## Benchmark: TravelRobeBench v0.1

Compare the candidate to at least three named travel/lounging garments of comparable coverage, warmth and price class; document exact models, sizes, market, price and purchase date. Also include a conventional robe and a travel coat/overshirt where useful, but don't compare unlike garments without declaring the use case.

### Wear and movement tasks

- standing, walking, sitting, reclined/seated travel posture, bending and reaching;
- sitting with a seatbelt, lap belt or bag strap in ordinary travel situations;
- putting on/taking off over base layers and outer layers;
- boarding-like step/aisle movement in a safe mock-up; use no live vehicle testing;
- accessing phone, passport, wallet, ticket and water bottle while seated and standing;
- stowing and retrieving a small carry-on item;
- keeping coverage and closure stable while walking and sitting;
- one-hand closure and pocket use where claimed;
- comfort over a preregistered wear duration with breaks and withdrawal always allowed.

### Material and care tests

- mass and packed volume;
- thermal/water-vapour/air permeability under relevant methods;
- pilling, abrasion, snagging, seam and closure durability;
- dimensional change, colour/appearance retention, wrinkle recovery and drying time;
- odour/cleaning assessment with a declared protocol; no antimicrobial/hygiene claim without appropriate tests;
- pocket seam loading and repeated closure cycles;
- repair time, tools, replacement component and cost.

### Human evaluation

Use wearer-led evaluation and, where feasible, randomized crossover comparisons. Capture tactile/thermal comfort, pressure points, coverage confidence, perceived elegance/style, mobility, seated comfort, pocket accessibility, ease of don/doff, privacy/peace-of-mind preferences and reasons for rejection separately. Do not collapse elegance or comfort into a universal model score.

Report participant variation, missingness, adverse experiences, withdrawals and subgroup outcomes. A single photograph or standing rating is not a travel-wear evaluation.

## Proposed targets — hypotheses, not results

- Zero accepted hard design constraints lost during pattern/assembly revisions.
- Zero pocket/closure critical failures in the frozen synthetic and bench-cycle corpus.
- At least 90% first-attempt completion of the declared don/doff and key item-access tasks in the tested participant group, with subgroup outcomes shown.
- At least 20% lower packed volume or garment mass than a matched reference only if comfort, durability and coverage remain non-inferior; select one endpoint after pilot.
- At least 20% reduction in care burden or drying time only after a frozen reference protocol and matched laundering.
- No meaningful decline in seated mobility, coverage stability or wearer-rated comfort against the matched reference.
- 100% traceability from approved design and material lot to construction plan and physical inspection.

Thresholds are provisional hypotheses to refine after a pilot; no product advantage is claimed.

## Adversarial / negative controls

1. Attractive render but no valid pattern or assembly plan.
2. Pocket is reachable standing but inaccessible while seated or under a seatbelt/bag strap.
3. Closure opens during bend/sit/walk task or cannot be operated one-handed when that feature is claimed.
4. Long hem or belt catches on luggage/wheel/step mock-up.
5. Deep pocket creates a pressure point or distorts drape.
6. Dark fabric appears opaque in studio but is insufficiently opaque under declared daylight conditions.
7. Fabric is labelled quick-dry but measured drying is worse than baseline.
8. Style revision changes mass, opacity, care, pocket or closure behavior and inherits stale evidence.
9. Unlicensed/culturally insensitive reference image or motif is silently used.
10. Robe is marketed as one-size-fits-all despite fit or coverage failures.
11. "Travel-safe" or anti-theft language is inferred from a hidden pocket alone.
12. Missing comfort or participant outcomes are silently excluded.

## First execution tranche

1. Audit #12093, #12157, #11731 and existing design-intent contracts.
2. Publish a versioned TravelRobeBrief and deterministic synthetic fixtures.
3. Produce 3 distinct silhouette/construction hypotheses before selecting materials.
4. Create a pocket/closure/coverage task map and seated-use acceptance protocol.
5. Build a materials shortlist from locally and internationally obtainable options with traceable supplier/lot claims.
6. Generate an editable pattern and typed sewing-plan fixture using the existing Atelier compilers.
7. Run synthetic semantic/adversarial checks before physical sampling.
8. Only after a sample exists, conduct wash, wear, packability and human acceptance trials.

## Claim ceiling

This issue establishes a research and design workstream only. A render is not a pattern; a pattern is not a comfortable robe; a hidden pocket is not security; a quick-dry declaration is not measured drying; a wash test is not lifetime; and a small wearer pilot does not establish universal comfort or cultural appropriateness.

