## CR-01 engineering refinement from the comfort-trajectory protocol

The draft protocol extension is in [PR #12220](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symthaea/pull/12220).

The critical geometric acceptance criterion should be a **posture-and-activity overlap/coverage envelope**, not a standing-only wrap measurement. Record the minimum overlap and any unintended opening in standing, seated, reclined, bend, reach and walking tasks. Test the concealed retention closure and external belt independently; the belt must not be the only thing keeping the wrap closed.

For the first sample:
- test seated pocket access and pressure with a bag strap and seatbelt mock-up;
- check sleeve, hem and belt interaction with steps and a stationary luggage-handle mock-up;
- verify that the robe remains complete and elegant without the warmth panel;
- test panel attachment pressure, shifting, removal and independent lap use.

Use a 120-minute initial wearer session; consider 180–240 minutes only after the short protocol is tolerable and the closure/fit gates pass. Dryrobe is a boundary comparator for technical changing/weather protection, not a like-for-like luxury travel wrap. MERSEA is a more direct wrap/portability comparator. Do not claim “travel-safe” or security performance from a hidden pocket.
